<!--
  An ordinary chart of accounts covering the groups the tool tests file their
  ledgers under.

  WHY THIS EXISTS. The tools that scope a ledger filter by group — outstanding,
  confirmations, GST, TDS, fixed assets — read the group collection so that a
  ledger in a SUB-group of the group asked for is still found. When a test
  registers no handler for that request, the mock 501s it, the fetch degrades to
  the old direct-parent match, and the test passes while exercising the fallback
  instead of the real path. Registering this keeps those suites on the path that
  ships.

  The hierarchy here is real but INERT: no ledger in any suite that loads this
  is filed below a group those suites ask for, so every result is identical to
  the old direct-parent behaviour and adding this fixture changed no assertion.
  Nesting that actually changes an answer is tested on its own chart in
  tests/tools/subGroupScoping.test.ts — putting it here would make unrelated
  suites' expectations depend on this file's shape.

  "Tangible Assets" is deliberately ABSENT even though Fixed Assets is present:
  fixedAssets.test.ts asks for it precisely to prove that a group name this
  company does not use returns an empty schedule with a warning rather than an
  error. Adding it would turn that test into something else.

  "SALARY PAYABLE" and "Unclassified" are groups some ledger fixtures name but
  Tally would not create; they are included so the chart is complete with
  respect to those fixtures rather than half-covering them.

  All values are invented. See tests/fixtures/README.md.
-->
<ENVELOPE>
 <HEADER>
  <VERSION>1</VERSION>
  <STATUS>1</STATUS>
 </HEADER>
 <BODY>
  <DESC>
   <CMPINFO>
    <COMPANY>0</COMPANY>
    <GROUP>0</GROUP>
   </CMPINFO>
  </DESC>
  <DATA>
   <COLLECTION ISMSTDEPTYPE="Yes" MSTDEPTYPE="7">
    <GROUP NAME="Current Assets" RESERVEDNAME="">
     <PARENT TYPE="String">Primary</PARENT>
